# Quad X baseline for the custom ArduPilot JSON SITL model.
#
# Nothing is inherited from copter.parm. SITL is launched with --defaults naming
# only this file and dds_smoke.parm, so everything not set here sits at the
# firmware default. That is the intended state, not an oversight: copter.parm
# targets a one to two kilogram vehicle on three cells, and two of its values
# would be actively wrong here - MOT_BAT_VOLT_MIN/MAX 9.6/12.8 against this
# airframe's 4S pack. Firmware default for both is 0.0, voltage thrust-limiting
# off, which is what we want.
#
# The 18 INS_ACC* parameters copter.parm carries are supplied instead by
# sim_defaults[] in libraries/SITL/SIM_JSON.cpp. Arming therefore depends on
# --model JSON: an exactly zero accel offset counts as uncalibrated, so another
# model would fail to arm with an INS error that never mentions the model.
#
# This airframe is 0.240 kg with a roll/pitch inertia of 0.00022 kg m^2 and a
# yaw inertia of 0.00038, so it reacts far faster than copter.parm's target.
# Attitude gains are deliberately still at their defaults; see
# workflow/backlog.md, and workflow/troubleshooting.md for the full audit.
FRAME_CLASS 1
FRAME_TYPE 1

# The SDF maps 1100-1900 us onto zero-to-full rotor speed, but ArduPilot's
# output range defaults to 1000-2000. Below 1100 the plugin computed a negative
# rotor speed, so a disarmed vehicle sat on the floor turning itself slowly and
# failed the compass field check ("Check mag field xy diff"). Matching the two
# ranges also stops throttle above 1900 from exceeding the modelled maximum.
MOT_PWM_MIN 1100
MOT_PWM_MAX 1900

# The simulated rotor produces thrust proportional to the square of commanded
# speed, and speed is linear in throttle, so full expo inverts that curve exactly.
MOT_THST_EXPO 1.0

# Hover throttle follows from the calibrated rotors rather than being guessed.
# At 0.306 kg the airframe needs 0.751 N per rotor against a 3.485 N maximum, a
# thrust fraction of 0.215, and MOT_THST_EXPO 1.0 makes the actuator output
# sqrt(0.215) = 0.46. Written down explicitly so the first flight of a test is
# deterministic instead of waiting for MOT_HOVER_LEARN.
#
# It was 0.41 while the model flew on the 0.240 kg the requirement asked for.
# The bill of materials with the LD06 says 0.306 kg, so thrust to weight is 4.6
# rather than 5.9.
MOT_THST_HOVER 0.46

# Attitude rate gains scaled for this airframe's control authority, which is
# what the stock gains get wrong. A full roll demand moves two rotors up and two
# down by half of max thrust each, so the peak roll moment is
# 2 * 3.485 N * 0.04525 m = 0.315 N m. Against the 0.00022 kg m^2 the airframe
# had before the lidar that was 82100 deg/s^2, or 7.8 times a ten-inch quad's.
# The LD06 on its mast takes roll inertia to roughly 0.00045, which halves that
# ratio - which is why the gains below were measured again rather than kept.
#
# The arm was 0.04455 m when these gains were set, from a 126 mm wheelbase that
# turned out to be an assumption; GEPRC publish 128 mm. The ratio moves from 7.7
# to 7.8, which is 1.6 per cent - far inside the accuracy of a scaling derived
# from one calculation, so the gains below are deliberately left alone. Redoing
# them for that would be false precision, and they are on the backlog to be
# measured by a sweep instead.
#
# Left at stock, the rate loop saturated: one rotor pinned at the MOT_SPIN_MIN
# floor of 597 rad/s while the others ran 1500 to 2200, the saturating set
# rotating between rotors. Copter then gave throttle away to keep attitude
# authority, so the log showed ThO 0.000 and a commanded descent of -2.5 m/s
# while the vehicle flung itself to 54 m. Defaults divided by 7.7; ArduPilot's
# own tuning guide also says smaller propellers need lower rate gains.
# Measured by scripts/sweep_rate_gains.sh rather than calculated, and measured
# again after the airframe gained the LD06. Adding 42 g on a mast 46 mm forward
# and 50 mm up roughly doubles the pitch inertia, so the gains that suited the
# 0.240 kg model no longer suit this one: at 0.027 the guided takeoff's worst
# tilt went from 6.5 to 24.0 degrees, against the check's 25 degree limit.
#
# The sweep at 0.306 kg puts the onset between 0.065 and 0.08. Ringing holds at
# 3 sign changes per run through 0.065, reaches 11 at 0.07, and at 0.08 the loop
# is gone: 146 sign changes and 62 per cent overshoot.
#
# 0.040 is a little under six tenths of that onset, the same margin the previous
# tuning used, and it beats 0.027 on every metric: overshoot 6.2 against 7.6 per
# cent, tracking error 1.08 against 1.25 degrees. 0.065 measured best of all and
# sits at 0.93 of the onset, which is no margin at all.
#
# The onset is optimistic. This simulation has no gyro noise, no frame resonance
# and no ESC lag beyond the motor model, and those are what force real gains
# lower. Treat 0.040 as an upper bound to approach on hardware, not a value to
# fly straight away.
ATC_RAT_RLL_P 0.040
ATC_RAT_RLL_I 0.040
ATC_RAT_RLL_D 0.0005
ATC_RAT_PIT_P 0.040
ATC_RAT_PIT_I 0.040
ATC_RAT_PIT_D 0.0005
ATC_RAT_YAW_P 0.023
ATC_RAT_YAW_I 0.0023

# The battery, and what to do when it runs out.
#
# Nothing was set here at all, which meant ArduPilot's own failsafe sat at its
# defaults - and its default is to do nothing. The demo node lands deliberately
# on a flat pack and says why, but it is a ROS node talking over DDS: if it
# stops, or the agent does, the vehicle would have gone on hovering until it
# fell. This is the layer underneath that, and it works whether or not anything
# is listening.
#
# The airframe carries a 4S LiHV pack, so the simulated one is told to be a 4S
# LiHV pack too. SITL's default is 12.6 V, which is a flat 4S and a full 3S, and
# against it every threshold below would either trip on the pad or never trip at
# all. 16.4 V is a 4S LiHV a little way into its charge.
# And the monitor has to exist before any of the rest of this means anything.
# AP_DDS publishes /ap/battery only for an instance the battery library calls
# healthy, and an unconfigured monitor is not healthy, so the topic simply never
# appeared - which is also how the demo node learned nothing was watching. 4 is
# the analog voltage-and-current monitor the SITL defaults use, and it is what
# feeds SIM_BATT_VOLTAGE through.
#
# This is the second thing that says the header of this file is optimistic:
# SITL is started with --defaults naming this file and the DDS one, so
# copter.parm is not loaded and nothing here is inherited from it.
BATT_MONITOR 4
SIM_BATT_VOLTAGE 16.4

# 3.5 V per cell is where a lithium cell has fallen off its plateau and a
# controlled descent is still a choice; 3.3 V is where it is not. Both act by
# landing rather than by returning: this vehicle flies indoors, has no idea
# where home is, and the shortest way to the floor is the only plan it can
# carry out.
BATT_LOW_VOLT 14.0
BATT_CRT_VOLT 13.2
BATT_FS_LOW_ACT 1
BATT_FS_CRT_ACT 1
# Ten seconds under the threshold before it acts, because a pack sags under
# throttle and recovers, and a failsafe that fires on the sag would land the
# vehicle every time it accelerated.
BATT_LOW_TIMER 10
